<Vertical id="postcard_demo" title="Postcard from History">

<!-- === Locations === -->

<Markdown id="loc_great_wall" title="Great Wall of China">
### Great Wall of China

![The Great Wall of China at Jinshanling](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg/330px-The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg)

The Great Wall stretches over 13,000 miles across northern China. Built over many centuries starting in the 7th century BC, it served as a defense against invasions from the north. Today it is one of the most visited landmarks in the world.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Wall_of_China)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_machu_picchu" title="Machu Picchu">
### Machu Picchu

![Machu Picchu](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg/330px-Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg)

This 15th-century Incan citadel sits high in the Andes Mountains of Peru, above the Sacred Valley. Built around 1450 AD, it was abandoned during the Spanish conquest and remained largely unknown to the outside world until 1911.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Picchu)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_pyramids" title="Pyramids of Giza">
### Pyramids of Giza

![Pyramids of the Giza Necropolis](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg/330px-Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg)

Built around 2570 BC near Cairo, Egypt, the Great Pyramid is the oldest of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World and the only one largely intact. The complex includes three pyramids and the Great Sphinx.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giza_pyramid_complex)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_colosseum" title="Colosseum">
### The Colosseum

![The Colosseum in Rome](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Colosseo_2020.jpg/330px-Colosseo_2020.jpg)

The Colosseum in Rome, Italy, is the largest ancient amphitheatre ever built. Completed in 80 AD, it could hold between 50,000 and 80,000 spectators for gladiatorial contests, animal hunts, and public spectacles.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_taj_mahal" title="Taj Mahal">
### Taj Mahal

![The Taj Mahal](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg/330px-Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg)

The Taj Mahal in Agra, India, is a white marble mausoleum built between 1632 and 1653 by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal. It is considered one of the finest examples of Mughal architecture.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taj_Mahal)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_altamira" title="Cave of Altamira">
### Cave of Altamira

![Bison painting in the Cave of Altamira](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/9_Bisonte_Magdaleniense_pol%C3%ADcromo.jpg/330px-9_Bisonte_Magdaleniense_pol%C3%ADcromo.jpg)

The Cave of Altamira near Santander in northern Spain contains some of the finest prehistoric art in the world, dating back roughly 15,000 years. The vivid paintings of bison, horses, and handprints on the cave ceiling were so remarkable that when first discovered in 1879, scholars refused to believe ancient humans could have created them.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Altamira)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_italica" title="Italica">
### Italica

![Amphitheatre at Italica](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Amphitheatre_Italica%2C_Spain.jpg/330px-Amphitheatre_Italica%2C_Spain.jpg)

Italica, just outside modern Seville, was founded in 206 BC by the Roman general Scipio Africanus to settle his veterans after defeating Hannibal's brother Hasdrubal at the Battle of Ilipa. It grew into one of the great cities of Roman Hispania and was the birthplace of emperors Trajan and Hadrian. Its massive amphitheatre, one of the largest in the Roman Empire, still stands today.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_mezquita" title="Mosque-Cathedral of Córdoba">
### Mosque-Cathedral of Córdoba

![Interior arches of the Mezquita](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Mezquita_de_C%C3%B3rdoba_desde_el_aire_%28C%C3%B3rdoba%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%29.jpg/330px-Mezquita_de_C%C3%B3rdoba_desde_el_aire_%28C%C3%B3rdoba%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%29.jpg)

The Mezquita of Córdoba began as a mosque in 784 AD and became the grandest in all of western Islam. Its forest of over 850 columns with red-and-white striped arches creates one of the most iconic interiors in architecture. After the Christian reconquest in 1236, a cathedral was built inside it -- creating a building unlike any other in the world.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosque%E2%80%93Cathedral_of_C%C3%B3rdoba)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_alhambra" title="Alhambra">
### The Alhambra

![The Alhambra from the Albaicín, Granada](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Dawn_Charles_V_Palace_Alhambra_Granada_Andalusia_Spain.jpg/330px-Dawn_Charles_V_Palace_Alhambra_Granada_Andalusia_Spain.jpg)

The Alhambra is a palace and fortress complex in Granada, built mainly between the 13th and 14th centuries by the Nasrid dynasty -- the last Muslim rulers in Spain. Its intricate carved stucco, tile mosaics, and tranquil courtyards represent the height of Islamic art in Europe. In 1492, it was surrendered to Ferdinand and Isabella, ending nearly 800 years of Muslim rule in the Iberian Peninsula.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alhambra)
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="loc_seville_cathedral" title="Seville Cathedral">
### Seville Cathedral

![Seville Cathedral and Giralda tower](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Southeast.jpg/330px-Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Southeast.jpg)

Seville Cathedral is the largest Gothic cathedral in the world, built between 1402 and 1506 on the site of a former Almohad mosque. Its builders reportedly declared, "Let us build a church so beautiful and so grand that those who see it will think we are mad." It houses the tomb of Christopher Columbus and stands beside the Giralda, a bell tower converted from the mosque's original minaret. During Spain's Age of Empire, Seville controlled all trade with the Americas.

[Learn more on Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seville_Cathedral)
</Markdown>

<!-- === Personas === -->

<Markdown id="persona_tourist" title="Tourist">
### Tourist

Visiting this landmark for the very first time. Everything is new and exciting. Wants to share what they see, hear, smell, and feel with a friend back home.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_local_child" title="Local Child">
### Local Child

Grew up near this landmark. It is part of everyday life -- visible on the way to school, woven into family stories, surrounded by tourists. It means something different when it is just *there*.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_archaeologist" title="Archaeologist">
### Archaeologist

A researcher who has spent years studying this site. Notices historical details, construction techniques, and cultural significance that most visitors walk right past.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_merchant" title="Merchant">
### Merchant (Historical)

A trader from centuries ago, passing through on a trade route. Sees the site as it was in its prime, bustling with activity, full of people buying and selling.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_restorer" title="Restorer">
### Restorer

Works on the conservation team that keeps this site from falling apart. Knows every crack, every patch, every spot where the rain gets in. Spends the day up on scaffolding or in storage rooms with tools most people have never seen. Sees this place from the inside -- literally.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_guard" title="Night Guard">
### Night Guard

Works the overnight shift. After the last visitors leave and the lights go down, this place belongs to them. The sounds are different at night. The shadows move. They know the building by feel as much as by sight.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_soldier" title="Soldier">
### Soldier (Historical)

Stationed here -- guarding a wall, a gate, or a border. This grand place is a workplace, and most days it is boring. Thinks about home, about the food, about the people left behind. A wonder of the world is just where they go to work.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_pilgrim" title="Pilgrim">
### Pilgrim (Historical)

Has traveled for weeks or months on foot to reach this place. It is not a tourist destination -- it is the purpose of the journey. May be seeking healing, forgiveness, knowledge, or trade. Has just arrived after a long road.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_spy" title="Spy">
### Spy (Historical)

Not who they say they are. Passing through this place gathering information -- about troop movements, trade routes, political alliances, or building techniques. Everything has a double meaning. The postcard should sound innocent but hint at what they are really paying attention to.
</Markdown>

<Markdown id="persona_artist" title="Artist">
### Artist

Has come here to sketch or paint. Sees this place in terms of light, shadow, line, and color. Notices things other visitors walk right past -- the way the stone catches the afternoon sun, the geometry of an archway, the texture of a worn step.
</Markdown>

<!-- === The Activity === -->

<Sequential id="postcard_activity" title="Postcard from History" launchable="true">

  <!-- Step 1: Choose location -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_1" title="Choose Your Location">
    <Markdown id="postcard_intro">
## Postcard from History

You will write a short postcard from the perspective of someone at a famous landmark. First, choose your location.
    </Markdown>
    <Carousel id="postcard_location" wrap="true" randomize="true">
      loc_great_wall, loc_machu_picchu, loc_pyramids, loc_colosseum, loc_taj_mahal, loc_altamira, loc_italica, loc_mezquita, loc_alhambra, loc_seville_cathedral
    </Carousel>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 2: Research your location -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_2" title="Research Notes">
    <Markdown id="postcard_research_intro">
## Research Your Location

You chose the **{{@postcard_location.title}}**. Your job is to figure out what it would *feel like* to actually be there. Try searching for travel blogs, photo galleries, or videos -- not just Wikipedia.

Jot down notes about:
- What you would **see** -- the landscape, the colors, the light, the crowds or emptiness
- What you would **hear, smell, or feel** -- wind, heat, dust, echoes, silence
- What the **place looks like up close** -- crumbling stone, steep steps, narrow paths, wide-open views
- Anything that **surprised you** -- something you wouldn't have guessed without looking it up
    </Markdown>
    <Collapsible id="postcard_research_example" title="See an example (Eiffel Tower)">
      <Markdown id="postcard_research_example_text">
**The Eiffel Tower -- research notes**

*From travel blogs and photos:*
* The base is HUGE up close -- the iron legs tower over you and you can see the rivets
* Long queues in the sun, people fanning themselves, vendors selling cold water
* The stairs are metal grating -- you can see straight down through them, which is terrifying
* Wind picks up as you climb; the whole structure hums and vibrates slightly
* At the top, Paris looks tiny and flat -- you can see for miles
* At night, 20,000 light bulbs sparkle for 5 minutes every hour -- people on the grass below cheer every time
* The park below smells like crepes and cigarette smoke
* Graffiti scratched into the iron railings -- names and dates from decades ago
      </Markdown>
    </Collapsible>
    <TextArea id="postcard_research_notes" rows="6" placeholder="Interesting facts, details, things I noticed..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 3: Choose persona -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_3" title="Choose Your Persona">
    <Markdown id="postcard_persona_header">
## Choose a Persona

Who will you be writing as at the **{{@postcard_location.title}}**?
    </Markdown>
    <Carousel id="postcard_persona" wrap="true" randomize="true">
      persona_tourist, persona_local_child, persona_archaeologist, persona_merchant, persona_restorer, persona_guard, persona_soldier, persona_pilgrim, persona_spy, persona_artist
    </Carousel>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 4: Develop your persona -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_4" title="Develop Your Persona">
    <Markdown id="postcard_persona_dev_intro">
## Develop Your Persona

You are a **{{@postcard_persona.title}}** at the **{{@postcard_location.title}}**. Invent a real human being. Give them a backstory:
- What's their name? Where are they from?
- Who are their friends and family? Who are they writing this postcard to?
- What do they do day-to-day?
- What would they notice or experience that others might miss?
    </Markdown>
    <Collapsible id="postcard_persona_example" title="See an example (Eiffel Tower peddler)">
      <Markdown id="postcard_persona_example_text">
**Peddler on the corner near the Eiffel Tower**

* Sells cheap replicas of the tower, keychains, and little flags
* Periodically chased away by police -- has to grab everything and run
* Spent a few months in jail over the years for pickpocketing and selling without a permit
* Two kids who sometimes beg on the street nearby
* Other parent's whereabouts unknown
* Sometimes harassed by locals who think peddlers make the area look bad
* Knows every shortcut and hiding spot within three blocks of the tower
* Has watched the tower light up thousands of times -- still thinks it's beautiful
* Speaks bits of five languages, just enough to make a sale
* Writing to a cousin back home who dreams of coming to Paris someday
      </Markdown>
    </Collapsible>
    <TextArea id="postcard_persona_notes" rows="6" placeholder="Who is this person? What's their life like? What do they care about?" />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 5: Write the postcard -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_5" title="Write Your Postcard">
    <Markdown id="postcard_write_instructions">
## Write Your Postcard

You are a **{{@postcard_persona.title}}** at the **{{@postcard_location.title}}**.

Write a short postcard (4-8 sentences) from this person's perspective. Use your research and persona notes to make it feel real. Include:
- What you see around you
- How you feel about being here
- A detail that only someone in your role would notice
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="postcard_draft" rows="8" placeholder="Dear friend, ..." />
    <Markdown id="postcard_draft_wordcount">{{wordcount(@postcard_draft.value) &lt; 20 ? "The submit button will become available once you have written enough to get feedback on." : "Keep going..."}}</Markdown>
    <ActionButton id="postcard_submit" label="Submit for Feedback" dependsOn="wordcount(@postcard_draft.value) >= 20 &amp;&amp; !@postcard_draft.readonly">
      <CopyFieldAction target="postcard_draft" output="postcard_final" />
      <LLMAction id="postcard_llm" target="postcard_feedback">
        You are giving feedback to a middle school student on a short postcard. Be warm, specific, and brief. Do NOT rewrite their text or suggest specific sentences. Only ask questions.

        The student is writing as a **<Ref target="postcard_persona" field="title" />** at the **<Ref target="postcard_location" field="title" />**.

        Their research notes about the location:
        <Ref target="postcard_research_notes" />

        Their persona development notes:
        <Ref target="postcard_persona_notes" />

        Read the student's draft carefully, along with their research and persona notes. Then ask 2-3 questions that would most help THIS student improve THIS postcard. Pick whatever matters most for their draft -- that might be:

        - Sensory detail (What did the stone feel like under your hands?)
        - Emotion or reaction (How did you feel when you first saw it?)
        - Narrative (Where did you run when the guard spotted you?)
        - Voice and persona (What would your character notice that a tourist wouldn't?)
        - Audience (Who is reading this postcard -- what would they want to hear about?)
        - Structure (You've got a great opening -- how do you want to end it?)
        - Using their research (Their notes mention steep terrain -- could the character react to that?)
        - Style (Could you slow down this moment and let the reader see it?)
        - Vocabulary (You wrote "went" -- is there a more vivid word for how your character moved?)
        - Length and focus (Is there a detail here worth expanding into two sentences?)

        The list above is just to show the range. Don't cover all of these -- pick the 2-3 short questions that would make the biggest difference for this particular student's draft. Keep each question brief -- one sentence is ideal. Make each question specific to what they wrote and what they researched. Do NOT write example sentences or suggest specific words. Just ask questions the student can answer by adding or reworking a sentence or two.

        Give feedback in up to three short sections:

        **Research** (only if needed)
        If the student's research notes are thin, empty, or mostly encyclopedic facts, briefly suggest what else to look for and where. Be specific to their location -- for example, for the Great Wall, you might suggest looking at photos of the hike up, close-ups of the crumbling stone, or travel blog descriptions of the terrain. For the Colosseum, you might suggest searching for what the tunnels underneath look like. Point them toward the kind of sensory, experiential details that would make their postcard more vivid. Keep this to 1-2 sentences. Skip this section entirely if their research notes already include good sensory and experiential detail.

        **Questions to think about** (2-3 bullets max)

        **Quick Fixes** (only if needed)
        Briefly note any grammar or spelling issues. Skip this section entirely if the writing is clean.

        Student's postcard:
        <Ref target="postcard_draft" />
      </LLMAction>
      <SetFieldAction target="postcard_draft" field="readonly" value="true" />
      <SetFieldAction target="postcard_location" field="readonly" value="true" />
      <SetFieldAction target="postcard_persona" field="readonly" value="true" />
    </ActionButton>
    <LLMFeedback id="postcard_feedback" />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 6: Revise -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_6" title="Revise Your Postcard">
    <Markdown id="postcard_revise_instructions">
## Revise Your Postcard

Review the feedback below and write your final version.

### Your Original Draft
    </Markdown>
    <Use ref="postcard_draft" />
    <Markdown id="postcard_feedback_header">
### Feedback
    </Markdown>
    <Use ref="postcard_feedback" />
    <Markdown id="postcard_final_header">
### Your Revised Postcard
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="postcard_final" rows="8" placeholder="Dear friend, ..." />
    <ActionButton id="postcard_final_submit" label="Submit Final Draft" dependsOn="wordcount(@postcard_final.value) >= 20 &amp;&amp; !@postcard_final.readonly">
      <SetFieldAction target="postcard_final" field="readonly" value="true" />
      <LLMAction id="llm_formal" target="voice_formal">
        A middle school student wrote the postcard below as a class exercise. We are showing them what their postcard would look like if adapted for a different audience. Rewrite it for a formal audience -- as if it were official correspondence or a letter to a dignitary. Change the greeting, sign-off, vocabulary, and sentence structure to suit the new recipient.

        <Ref target="postcard_final" />
      </LLMAction>
      <LLMAction id="llm_academic" target="voice_academic">
        A middle school student wrote the postcard below as a class exercise. We are showing them what their postcard would look like if adapted for a different audience. Rewrite it for an academic audience -- as if it were being shared with researchers. Use precise, analytical language appropriate for readers with expertise in history or cultural studies.

        <Ref target="postcard_final" />
      </LLMAction>
      <LLMAction id="llm_child" target="voice_child">
        A middle school student wrote the postcard below as a class exercise. We are showing them what their postcard would look like if adapted for a different audience. Rewrite it as it would be if it were being sent to a young child (age 6-7), so they could understand and enjoy reading it. Keep the same sender, but they're now writing to a young relative. Use simple words, short sentences, and explain any big ideas in a way a small child would find exciting.

        <Ref target="postcard_final" />
      </LLMAction>
      <LLMAction id="llm_literary" target="voice_literary">
        A middle school student wrote the postcard below as a class exercise. We are showing them what their postcard would look like if adapted for a different audience. Rewrite it as it would sound if being placed by an author on their travel blog, for readers who appreciate literary prose. Use vivid imagery, sensory detail, and varied sentence rhythm.

        <Ref target="postcard_final" />
      </LLMAction>
      <LLMAction id="llm_business" target="voice_business">
        A middle school student wrote the postcard below as a class exercise. We are showing them what their postcard would look like if adapted for a different audience. Rewrite it as if it were being sent to a business colleagues -- as a concise travel update or professional trip memorandum. Focus on key observations, practical takeaways, and a professional tone.

        <Ref target="postcard_final" />
      </LLMAction>
    </ActionButton>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 7: Explore different voices -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_7" title="Your Postcard in Different Voices">
    <Markdown id="postcard_voices_intro">
## Writing for Different Audiences

Earlier, you chose *who* was writing -- a tourist, a child, an archaeologist. That was about the sender's perspective.

Now let's think about the *recipient*. The same message changes depending on who you are writing *for*. Compare how your postcard reads when adapted for a young child, for academic readers, or for business colleagues. The content stays the same, but the vocabulary, sentence structure, and tone shift to meet the audience where they are.
    </Markdown>
    <Use ref="postcard_final" />
    <Tabs id="postcard_voices">
      <LLMFeedback id="voice_formal" title="Formal" />
      <LLMFeedback id="voice_academic" title="Academic" />
      <LLMFeedback id="voice_child" title="Child" />
      <LLMFeedback id="voice_literary" title="Literary" />
      <LLMFeedback id="voice_business" title="Business" />
    </Tabs>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- Step 8: Export -->
  <Vertical id="postcard_step_8" title="Export Your Work">
    <Markdown id="postcard_export_intro">
## Export Your Postcard

Review your work below, then export it as a PDF.
    </Markdown>
    <ActionButton id="postcard_export" label="Export as PDF">
      <PrintAction id="postcard_print" />
    </ActionButton>

    <!-- Page 1: Choices + final draft -->
    <Markdown id="export_page1">
### Where and Who

**Location:** {{@postcard_location.title}}

**Persona:** {{@postcard_persona.title}}

---

### Final Postcard

{{@postcard_final.value}}
    </Markdown>

    <!-- Page 2: Research + persona development -->
    <Markdown id="export_page2" class="print-page-break">
### Research Notes

{{@postcard_research_notes.value}}

---

### Persona Notes

{{@postcard_persona_notes.value}}
    </Markdown>

    <!-- Page 3: Draft + feedback -->
    <Markdown id="export_page3" class="print-page-break">
### Original Draft

{{@postcard_draft.value}}

---

### Feedback

{{@postcard_feedback.value}}
    </Markdown>

  </Vertical>

</Sequential>

</Vertical>
